Subject: [PATCH 54/54] arch/unix: write-file -- the hosted firmware authors a host file

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

TODO 20. The one target with a host filesystem could READ from it and never
WRITE to it. read_dictionary() loads the .dict; `-f` opens a disk image
O_RDONLY; and that was the whole of it -- write_dictionary() is #if 0, the blk
package exposes read-blocks with no write-blocks, and arch/unix has no nvram
backend (only the QEMU arches do). So a binary structure that dsl/struct.fth or
dsl/elf.fth assembled in the Forth arena evaporated when the process exited.

That is exactly the gap REVIEW-preboot-forth-as-a-poke-engine.md G6 named: "the
reader is still ahead of the writer". This binds one primitive that lets a
structure the Forth built OUTLIVE the process as a real file:

    write-file ( data-adr data-len fname-adr fname-len -- actual-len )

HOSTED-ONLY ON PURPOSE. The bind is in arch/unix's arch_init(), not in the
common libopenbios/init.c beside le-l!/le-w!, because only a hosted firmware has
a host filesystem to write to; a bare-metal target would have no file to name.
This is the writer counterpart to read_dictionary, so it sits beside it.

IT NAMES EVERY FAILURE and returns the bytes actually written (-1 if it never
opened the file), rather than reporting a success it did not achieve -- a
firmware word that writes nothing and says nothing is the silent liar the repo's
CLAUDE.md exists to hunt. It cannot carry strerror(errno): config.h #defines
`errno` to the firmware's own errno_int (declared only #if __APPLE__), which the
host's open()/write() never touch -- so it names the path and the operation
instead. The verdict is external and un-fakeable: examples/.../dsl/elf-write.fth
authors a 132-byte static x86-64 ELF, write-file persists it, and
smoke-openbios.sh's file-writer track has the KERNEL run it -- the exit status
must equal the code the Forth authored. A file that runs is not a file the
firmware merely claims it wrote.

diff --git a/arch/unix/unix.c b/arch/unix/unix.c
--- a/arch/unix/unix.c
+++ b/arch/unix/unix.c
@@ -179,6 +179,86 @@ static ucell read_dictionary(char *fil)
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). write-file: the firmware AUTHORS a host file.
+ *
+ * The hosted target has always been able to READ and never to WRITE. It loads
+ * its dictionary (read_dictionary, above), and `-f` opens a disk image
+ * O_RDONLY (see diskemu) -- but nothing the Forth built could ever outlive the
+ * process. dsl/struct.fth and dsl/elf.fth already assemble a binary structure
+ * in the Forth arena; this is the one primitive that lets the result LEAVE.
+ *
+ * It is the poke model's missing half. REVIEW-preboot-forth-as-a-poke-engine.md
+ * §G6 records that "the reader is still ahead of the writer": the firmware could
+ * snapshot and diff a region but had no way to persist one. And it exists ONLY
+ * on this target -- bind_func() below is in arch_init(), not in the common
+ * libopenbios/init.c beside le-l! -- because only a HOSTED firmware has a host
+ * filesystem to write to. A bare-metal target would have no file() to name.
+ *
+ *   write-file ( data-adr data-len fname-adr fname-len -- actual-len )
+ *
+ * Returns the number of bytes ACTUALLY written; -1 on a failure that never
+ * opened the file at all. A short write returns the short count rather than
+ * lying with the requested length -- the caller compares actual-len to len and
+ * decides. Every failure path NAMES itself through printk with the path and the
+ * operation that failed: a firmware word that writes nothing and reports success
+ * is exactly the silent liar this repo exists to hunt (CLAUDE.md, "Fix the liar
+ * first"). It cannot carry strerror(errno) because config.h #defines `errno` to
+ * the firmware's own errno_int, which host open()/write() never touch.
+ */
+static void forth_write_file(void)
+{
+	int fnlen = (int)(cell)POP();
+	char *fnadr = (char *)cell2pointer(POP());
+	int dlen = (int)(cell)POP();
+	const char *dadr = (const char *)cell2pointer(POP());
+	char path[4096];
+	int fd, done = 0;
+	ssize_t n;
+
+	if (fnlen <= 0 || fnlen >= (int)sizeof(path)) {
+		printk("write-file: refusing a filename of %d bytes"
+		       " (must be 1..%d)\n", fnlen, (int)sizeof(path) - 1);
+		PUSH((ucell)(cell)-1);
+		return;
+	}
+	memcpy(path, fnadr, fnlen);
+	path[fnlen] = '\0';
+
+	if (dlen < 0) {
+		printk("write-file: refusing a negative length %d for '%s'\n",
+		       dlen, path);
+		PUSH((ucell)(cell)-1);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	fd = open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
+	if (fd < 0) {
+		printk("write-file: cannot open '%s' for writing"
+		       " (does its directory exist, and is it writable?)\n",
+		       path);
+		PUSH((ucell)(cell)-1);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	while (done < dlen) {
+		n = write(fd, dadr + done, (size_t)(dlen - done));
+		if (n < 0) {
+			printk("write-file: write to '%s' failed after %d/%d"
+			       " bytes\n", path, done, dlen);
+			break;
+		}
+		if (n == 0)
+			break;
+		done += (int)n;
+	}
+
+	if (close(fd) < 0)
+		printk("write-file: close of '%s' failed"
+		       " -- the file may be short\n", path);
+
+	PUSH((ucell)(cell)done);
+}
+
 
 /*
  * functions used by primitives
@@ -537,6 +617,9 @@ arch_init( void )
 
 	device_end();
         bind_func("platform-boot", boot);
+        /* mklab: hosted-only. Author a binary in the arena, persist it to a
+         * real host file. See forth_write_file() above and TODO §20. */
+        bind_func("write-file", forth_write_file);
 }
 
 int
